---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import LoanCalculator from '@/components/tools/finance/LoanCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="loan-calculator">
  <LoanCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the loan calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Enter the <strong>loan amount</strong>: the sum you receive, not the total you will repay.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter the <strong>annual interest rate</strong>. If your offer gives an APR that includes
        fees, put the interest rate here and the fee in the fee field, so the two stay separate.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set the <strong>term</strong> in years, with extra months if your offer is something like 42
        or 54 months.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you want, add an <strong>extra monthly principal payment</strong> to see the interest saved
        and the months removed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read the payment, the total interest and the cost of borrowing, then export the full
        schedule as CSV.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What the calculator is modelling</h2>
    <p>
      The loan calculator models a standard amortising loan: a fixed rate, equal monthly payments, and
      a balance that reaches zero on the final payment. That structure covers personal loans, most
      student loans, home improvement loans, business term loans and almost all car finance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each month the lender charges interest on the balance at the start of the month. The rest of your
      payment reduces the principal. The balance falls a little each month, so the interest charge falls
      too, and the principal part of a fixed payment grows. That is amortisation, and the schedule below
      the calculator shows it line by line.
    </p>

    <h2>Monthly payment per 10,000 borrowed</h2>
    <p>
      Multiply by your loan amount in units of ten thousand. A 25,000 loan at 10% over five years is
      2.5 × 212.47 = 531.18 a month.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Rate</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">1 year</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">2 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">3 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">5 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">7 years</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>4%</td><td style="text-align:right">851.50</td><td style="text-align:right">434.25</td><td style="text-align:right">295.24</td><td style="text-align:right">184.17</td><td style="text-align:right">136.69</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6%</td><td style="text-align:right">860.66</td><td style="text-align:right">443.21</td><td style="text-align:right">304.22</td><td style="text-align:right">193.33</td><td style="text-align:right">146.09</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8%</td><td style="text-align:right">869.88</td><td style="text-align:right">452.27</td><td style="text-align:right">313.36</td><td style="text-align:right">202.76</td><td style="text-align:right">155.86</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10%</td><td style="text-align:right">879.16</td><td style="text-align:right">461.45</td><td style="text-align:right">322.67</td><td style="text-align:right">212.47</td><td style="text-align:right">166.01</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12%</td><td style="text-align:right">888.49</td><td style="text-align:right">470.73</td><td style="text-align:right">332.14</td><td style="text-align:right">222.44</td><td style="text-align:right">176.53</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15%</td><td style="text-align:right">902.58</td><td style="text-align:right">484.87</td><td style="text-align:right">346.65</td><td style="text-align:right">237.90</td><td style="text-align:right">192.97</td></tr>
          <tr><td>18%</td><td style="text-align:right">916.80</td><td style="text-align:right">499.24</td><td style="text-align:right">361.52</td><td style="text-align:right">253.93</td><td style="text-align:right">210.18</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20%</td><td style="text-align:right">926.35</td><td style="text-align:right">508.96</td><td style="text-align:right">371.64</td><td style="text-align:right">264.94</td><td style="text-align:right">222.06</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Total interest per 10,000 borrowed</h2>
    <p>
      The same loans, as the total interest paid. This number shows why a longer term is expensive,
      even when the payment looks comfortable.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Rate</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">1 year</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">2 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">3 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">5 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">7 years</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>4%</td><td style="text-align:right">218</td><td style="text-align:right">422</td><td style="text-align:right">629</td><td style="text-align:right">1,050</td><td style="text-align:right">1,482</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8%</td><td style="text-align:right">439</td><td style="text-align:right">855</td><td style="text-align:right">1,281</td><td style="text-align:right">2,166</td><td style="text-align:right">3,092</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12%</td><td style="text-align:right">662</td><td style="text-align:right">1,298</td><td style="text-align:right">1,957</td><td style="text-align:right">3,347</td><td style="text-align:right">4,828</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15%</td><td style="text-align:right">831</td><td style="text-align:right">1,637</td><td style="text-align:right">2,480</td><td style="text-align:right">4,274</td><td style="text-align:right">6,209</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20%</td><td style="text-align:right">1,116</td><td style="text-align:right">2,215</td><td style="text-align:right">3,379</td><td style="text-align:right">5,896</td><td style="text-align:right">8,653</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      At 20%, a 10,000 loan over seven years instead of three has more than double the interest (8,653
      against 3,379), but the payment is only about 40% lower. The shortest term you can comfortably
      afford is almost always the cheapest.
    </p>

    <h2>Interest rate, APR and the cost of borrowing</h2>
    <p>
      The interest rate is the price of the money. The APR also includes compulsory fees, as an annual
      rate, so it is the better number to compare two offers with different fees. The total cost of
      borrowing (interest plus fees, in currency, not percent) tells you what the loan costs, and this
      calculator shows it next to the payment.
    </p>
    <p>
      On each quote, check if interest is charged on the reducing balance, as modelled here, or as a
      flat rate on the original amount for the whole term. Flat-rate quotes look cheap, but are usually
      close to double the equivalent reducing-balance rate.
    </p>

    <h2>Accuracy</h2>
    <p>
      All arithmetic uses arbitrary-precision decimals and rounds to the minor currency unit exactly
      once per payment, as servicers do. The final instalment takes the rounding residue, so the
      balance ends at zero. Results match a standard fixed-rate statement to the penny. Lenders that use
      daily interest accrual, actual-day counts or a different rounding convention give small
      differences.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
